---
import type { GetStaticPaths } from 'astro';
import { getCollection, render } from 'astro:content';
import { Picture, getImage } from 'astro:assets';
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Plate from '../../components/Plate.astro';
import { bodyWidth, bodySizes, EXPANDED_WIDTH } from '../../layout';

export const getStaticPaths = (async () => {
  const projects = await getCollection('projects');
  return projects.map((project) => ({ params: { slug: project.id }, props: { project } }));
}) satisfies GetStaticPaths;

const { project } = Astro.props;
const { title, summary, credit, spec, year, images } = project.data;
const { Content } = await render(project);

/* Frames 1..n, then the hero again LAST and at its native aspect.

   It is not a repeat. The thing at the top of this page is a 21:9 band cut out
   of the hero, and closing the sequence with the whole image is what that band
   was taken from — the crop opens the page, the full frame resolves it. Last
   rather than first because first would put it directly under its own crop,
   with only the prose between, which reads as the image failing to load rather
   than as a sequence.

   `images` is `.min(1)` in the schema, so this is never empty. A one-image
   Project is exactly its hero shown uncropped, which is the right answer. */
const frames = [...images.slice(1), images[0]];

/* The Expanded View's source, one per Frame, resolved at build time so the
   markup can be a plain link.

   `layout: 'none'` for the reason Base.astro spells out on the Social Card:
   astro.config.mjs sets a global 'constrained' default that applies to getImage
   too, and without this each of these becomes a six-width srcset where exactly
   one URL is read.

   Clamped to the source width so a portrait Frame is never upscaled. Every
   committed asset is 3200 px on its LONG edge (docs/asset-delivery.md §2), so a
   tall one can be well under 2000 px wide and sharp would happily enlarge it. */
const expanded = await Promise.all(
  frames.map((frame) =>
    getImage({
      src: frame.src,
      width: Math.min(EXPANDED_WIDTH, frame.src.width),
      format: 'webp',
      layout: 'none',
    }),
  ),
);
---

<Base title={`${title} | Coulter Heiberger`} description={summary} image={images[0]}>
  <main>
    {/* The hero leads. This page IS the Plate in its second presentation, and
        putting the work first is the same argument content-architecture.md §1
        makes for `/` not being a landing page.

        It also keeps the page image-LCP, which is the regime ADR-0002 costed
        the ~220 KB hero crossover in. Three hundred pixels of heading above it
        would hand the LCP to an <h1> on mobile and quietly move the page into
        the other regime.

        NO data-anim, and no Registration: first screen. */}
    <div class="hero">
      <Plate frame={images[0]} slug={project.id} context="detail" priority />
    </div>

    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p class="summary">{summary}</p>

    {/* Credit and the Specification Line together — provenance and
        construction, the pair CONTEXT.md defines against each other. `credit`
        appears here and nowhere else on the site (content-architecture.md §2). */}
    <dl class="facts">
      <div>
        <dt class="t-label">Credit</dt>
        <dd class="t-spec">{credit}</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="t-label">Year</dt>
        <dd class="t-spec">{year}</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="t-label">Specification</dt>
        <dd class="t-spec">{spec}</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>

    <div class="prose" data-anim="rise">
      <Content />
    </div>

    {/* The gallery. Frames at NATIVE ASPECT — no crop, no `framing`, because
        nothing here is being fitted to a band: the two art-directed crops exist
        for the Plate, which has a layout to satisfy, and a Frame does not.

        No Registration on any of them. An image the visitor scrolled to is not
        information arriving, it is the thing they came to see — and twelve
        wipes in a column is precisely the "performing" the motion spec exists
        to refuse.

        No captions and no process/finished label. ~15 of the 47 Frames are
        working images rather than finished camera renders and they render
        identically; the body prose above makes that argument better than a
        badge would. See content-architecture.md §4.

        EACH FRAME IS A LINK TO ITS OWN FULL-SIZE FILE, and Base.astro's script
        intercepts the click into the Expanded View. A link rather than a button
        on purpose: with scripting off, or on a cmd-click, it still does the
        thing a visitor wanted, and its accessible name comes from the alt text
        already on the image inside it. A <button> here would be a control that
        does nothing when the script does not run, and lighthouserc.cjs asserts
        `link-name` and `button-name` alike without being able to tell the
        difference. */}
    <ul class="gallery">
      {frames.map((frame, i) => (
        <li>
          <a class="expand" href={expanded[i].src}>
            {/* <Picture>, and here it IS the right tool — which is worth saying
                because Plate.astro argues at length for hand-assembling one.
                That argument is about COMPOSITION: a Plate needs a different
                crop per breakpoint, and <Picture> switches format, not framing.
                A Frame is one crop at one aspect and needs exactly the format
                switch, so the component that does only that is the correct one.

                AVIF WITH A WEBP FALLBACK, where this was WebP alone. The Plate
                pipeline has always led with AVIF and the gallery never did, and
                on mobile that is not a cosmetic gap: `loading="lazy"` does not
                mean "not now" — Chrome's lazy threshold widens as the connection
                slows, so on the throttled 4G the perf gate emulates, the first
                few Frames are fetched WHILE the hero is still arriving and take
                bandwidth from the LCP element. Cecret's terrain map was the
                largest single asset on its page at 199.1 KB, landing at 2.8s.
                Measured through sharp at the width actually served, AVIF q50
                against WebP q80: 199.1 KB -> 93.6 KB, and 122.4 -> 57.4 at the
                rung below. A 53% cut on the thing that was crowding the hero. */}
            <Picture
              src={frame.src}
              alt={frame.alt}
              width={bodyWidth}
              sizes={bodySizes}
              formats={['avif']}
              fallbackFormat="webp"
              layout="constrained"
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
            />
          </a>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    {/* ONE dialog per page, not one per Frame. The script sets its src from the
        link that was clicked, so the file is fetched on demand and nothing is
        downloaded for a Frame nobody opens — a Gate in CONTEXT.md's sense.

        The <img> ships with no src and an empty alt, and both are replaced at
        open time. Empty rather than absent because an image with no alt is an
        `image-alt` failure the moment it has a src, and the window between
        those two assignments is not somewhere to be clever.

        Close is a word, not a glyph: it needs no aria-label, it renders in the
        specification line's own type role, and there is no icon set on this
        site for it to be the first member of. */}
    <dialog class="expanded">
      <button type="button" class="t-spec close" data-close>Close</button>
      <img alt="" />
    </dialog>
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  /* ONE DATUM EDGE. Everything on this page — hero, heading, facts, prose and
     every Frame — starts on the same left edge, and only the right edges vary.
     That is the literal reading of the direction's own name, and it is what
     makes Cecret's seven near-identical terrain masks read as a sequence rather
     than as seven competing heroes. It is also what gives a 5-Frame Project and
     a 12-Frame Project the same rhythm.

     Recorded honestly: this is a READING of docs/design-direction.md, not a
     measurement. The repo holds no layout geometry from the precedent analysis
     — the prototype is gone (issue #11) — so the only numbers behind it are
     tokens.css and the page shell. */
  main {
    max-width: var(--measure-page);
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding: var(--rhythm) var(--gutter);
  }

  .hero {
    margin-bottom: 40px;
  }

  /* ONE COLUMN BELOW THE HERO. Heading, summary, prose and every Frame share
     --body-col, which is half the page shell. The hero above runs the full
     shell, so the page is one wide plate over one half-width column, both
     starting on the same left edge.

     This replaced two measures with one: the text used to sit at --measure
     (64ch) and the Frames at --gallery-col (1080px), which drew two right edges
     down the page for no reason a reader could see. What it costs the reading
     measure is recorded on the token itself, in tokens.css. */
  h1 {
    font-size: var(--t-h2);
    line-height: 1.02;
    max-width: var(--body-col);
  }

  .summary {
    margin: 20px 0 0;
    max-width: var(--body-col);
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .facts {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 12px 48px;
    margin: 40px 0 0;
    padding: 20px 0 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .facts div {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 6px;
  }

  .facts dd {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .prose {
    margin-top: var(--rhythm);
    max-width: var(--body-col);
  }

  /* Capped by the same token layout.ts composes `sizes` from, so the width the
     CSS draws and the width the browser fetches cannot drift apart. */
  .gallery {
    list-style: none;
    margin: var(--rhythm) 0 0;
    padding: 0;
    max-width: var(--body-col);
  }

  .gallery li + li {
    margin-top: 40px;
  }

  /* The link is a block wrapper and nothing else. No underline, no border, no
     colour change: base.css gives every <a> `color: inherit` and there is no
     text inside this one to distinguish. The cursor is the affordance. */
  .expand {
    display: block;
  }

  /* <picture> is inline by default, which would put a descender's worth of gap
     under every Frame inside its block-level link. */
  .gallery :global(picture) {
    display: block;
  }

  .gallery :global(img) {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }

  /* ---- the Expanded View -------------------------------------------------
     CONTEXT.md names it. One Frame at the size of the window, over the rest of
     the page blurred back.

     THE BLUR IS ON THE DIALOG, NOT ON ::backdrop, and that is not a stylistic
     choice. Custom properties only began inheriting into ::backdrop recently
     and unevenly, and where they do not, `var(--ground)` resolves to nothing
     and the UA's own translucent black lands over a paper site. A real element
     resolves the token normally in every engine. So the dialog is sized to the
     whole viewport, carries the ground itself, and ::backdrop is cleared out
     from under it.

     `overflow: hidden` because backdrop-filter establishes a containing block
     and a large Frame must not be able to scroll the page underneath. */
  dialog.expanded {
    width: 100vw;
    height: 100dvh;
    max-width: 100vw;
    max-height: 100dvh;
    margin: 0;
    border: 0;
    padding: var(--gutter);
    overflow: hidden;
    background: color-mix(in oklab, var(--ground) 82%, transparent);
    /* Safari needed the prefix until 18. The design degrades to a plain tint
       without it, which is legible but not the effect. */
    -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(24px);
    backdrop-filter: blur(24px);
  }

  dialog.expanded::backdrop {
    background: transparent;
  }

  /* LOCK THE DOCUMENT WHILE THE VIEW IS OPEN. `showModal()` makes the rest of
     the page inert, but it does NOT stop it scrolling — measured here, not
     assumed: with the dialog open, one PageDown moved the document from 3233 to
     4020 while the image stayed put, so the visitor closes the view and finds
     themselves somewhere else on the page.

     `:has()` is the right tool and this is the case docs/styling.md permits it
     for: a state toggle, not structure. Nothing is laid out by this rule; it
     only stops something moving.

     It also removes the scrollbar, which makes the dialog's own `100vw` exact.
     While the bar is present `100vw` includes it, so the dialog would overhang
     by its width and put a horizontal scrollbar on the document underneath. One
     rule, both problems. */
  :global(html:has(dialog.expanded[open])) {
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  /* [open] rather than the bare selector: the UA rule is `dialog:not([open])
     { display: none }`, so declaring `display` unconditionally would leave the
     dialog on the page permanently. */
  dialog.expanded[open] {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-rows: auto minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 16px;
    justify-items: end;
  }

  dialog.expanded img {
    justify-self: center;
    align-self: center;
    max-width: 100%;
    max-height: 100%;
    width: auto;
  }

  /* --ink, not the --muted that .t-spec supplies. This is the only control in
     the view, it sits over a translucent ground whose effective contrast is
     whatever image happens to be behind it, and 4.90:1 against a flat ground is
     not a number that survives that. --ink is 15.6:1 and survives anything. */
  .close {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 8px 0;
    border: 0;
    background: none;
    color: var(--ink);
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  /* A short fade, declared only under no-preference, so `reduce` gets the
     dialog appearing with no transition rather than a transition switched off.
     Same inversion as every other move on this site.

     `allow-discrete` and @starting-style are what let an element that goes
     display:none animate at all. Where they are unsupported the dialog simply
     appears and disappears, which is the correct failure. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    dialog.expanded {
      opacity: 0;
      transition:
        opacity 0.24s ease-out,
        display 0.24s ease-out allow-discrete,
        overlay 0.24s ease-out allow-discrete;
    }

    dialog.expanded[open] {
      opacity: 1;
    }

    @starting-style {
      dialog.expanded[open] {
        opacity: 0;
      }
    }
  }
</style>
